# Buttons and editable fields

Use the shared classes in `apps/web/styles/globals.css`. Do not invent a text-only control for an action.

## Actions

A control that adds, removes, opens, saves, or commits is a button.

- `btn` is the default: bordered, surface background, `var(--ink-2)` text, weight 600.
- `btn btn-sm` sits in a table, a list row, or beside body copy.
- `btn btn-primary` is the single action that completes the screen. One per view.
- A state control (`Pending`, `Open`, `Draft`) is `btn btn-sm` plus a state class. The state class changes color only. The border and background stay.

```tsx
<button type="button" className="btn btn-sm">Add a point</button>
<button type="button" className="btn btn-primary">Commit allocation</button>
<button type="button" className="btn btn-sm icm-state pending">Pending</button>
```

Never style an action as unbordered colored text. That reads as a link. Links are `<a>` elements that navigate.

## In-place values

A fact the user can edit in place is a field, not a button and not a link. Use `field-edit`: a border, the surface background, inherited type, and a text cursor. Hover darkens the border. Click replaces it with an input of the same width. Enter or blur saves. Escape cancels.

Section navigation is a nav list. It is not `btn` and not `field-edit`.
